WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.040
Let’s say you’re trying to
find some solid information

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.540
about a serious health
problem that concerns you,

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.120
like high blood pressure,
or diabetes.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.760
Yet, everywhere you look,

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.480
someone’s trying to sell you
something like vitamins,

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.720
yoga mats, blenders, drugs.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.360
Well, breathe a
sigh of relief

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.440
because all we bring
you are the facts.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.800
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.200
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.560
Today we continue with
part two of our series on yoga

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:31.880
and we start with the question:

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:36.240
which of the 50 different yoga styles
have been shown to be best?

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.727
Should doctors recommend
their patients do yoga?

00:00:39.727 --> 00:00:41.320
As you've seen in my last videos,
the difficulties associated

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.480
with recommending yoga
stem from the low quality

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.760
of scientific evidence available
regarding its effects.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.520
Oddly, this lack of evidence is
partly due to a common failure

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.360
among researchers to define
what they actually studied.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.440
They just say yoga without
defining what they mean.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.400
And different types of yoga differ
greatly in what they demand

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.160
in terms of physical strength,
depth of meditation,

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.840
breathing control, and
spiritual component.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.000
Yet it's very common for
reports of studies not to define

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.520
which type they used.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.680
This not only makes it hard to
compare results between studies,

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.360
it also makes it very hard
to translate any findings

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:20.560
to the bedside
when counseling patients.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.480
Yoga is broadly defined
as a mind-body practice

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.440
composed of physical postures,

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.160
breathing techniques,
and meditation,

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.160
and so if researchers say
they put yoga to the test,

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:35.520
you'd assume all three components
would be present.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.560
However some studies use
meditation only and call it yoga,

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.560
with no postures or breathing.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:45.480
Others only examine breathing
exercises and call it yoga.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:49.320
Others consider yoga only postures,
and still others say they're studying

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.560
forms of yoga without
any of the three components.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.760
And so if a study
shows no benefit,

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:59.360
does that really mean yoga as
commonly practiced didn't help?

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.040
And if a study does show benefit,

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.200
what exactly do you tell
patients to do to achieve it?

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.800
We can take some comfort
in the fact that a review

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.200
entitled "Is one yoga style
better than another?"

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.480
attempted to compare
the effects of the more than

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.000
50 different yoga styles
used in hundreds of trials,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.640
and they concluded that there
was no apparent winner

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.840
in terms of whether
the results turned out positive,

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.160
so to a certain extent they
may be interchangeable.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:29.960
I've talked about the need
to have active control to see

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:33.680
if there's some benefit to yoga
beyond just the exercise component,

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.680
but such controls may still
not deal with confounders

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.320
such as socialization.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.000
Yoga usually involves
going to classes.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.640
So you can imagine if you were
studying the effects of yoga

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.720
on something like depression,
does breaking routine,

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.120
getting out, and
meeting other people

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.520
have any effect,
independent of the yoga?

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:52.560
Sadly for depression,

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:56.640
yoga doesn't even seem to have
a benefit compared to doing nothing.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.760
Yoga does appear
to be helpful with anxiety,

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.560
but not for people anxious
to the extent

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.680
that they're actually diagnosed
with an anxiety disorder.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.680
And all the anxiety benefit
from yoga appears to just be

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.280
from the physical activity,
since there appears to be

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.800
no benefit compared
to other types of exercise.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.680
So unfortunately, yoga does
not appear to be effective

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.600
for mood and anxiety disorders.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.400
A review on the effects of yoga
on depressive symptoms

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.320
in people with mental
disorders in general,

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.720
including conditions like
PTSD or schizophrenia,

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.640
found no significant effect compared
to things like social support

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.920
that offer the same kind
of attention, or to doing nothing,

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:42.440
but did find an effect compared
to so-called "waitlist" controls.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:46.520
See, unlike in studies of drug effects
where a placebo sugar pill

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.720
is relatively straightforward,
selection of a control condition

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:54.400
is much more complex for
behavioral interventions like yoga,

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.480
and one common solution is
a passive control such as a waitlist.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:02.040
Basically, people are randomized
to either yoga or onto a waitlist

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:05.120
where they'll still get yoga
but have to wait awhile.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:07.360
Now that's better than having
no control group at all,

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.320
since it still controls
for the passage of time,

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:11.160
meaning whether or not

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:12.560
your condition would
have just gotten better

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:17.600
naturally on its own, but doesn't
really control for the placebo effect.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.120
If instead you were just told
you were entering a study

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:22.160
to see if some exercises may help,

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.560
and then unbeknownst to you
randomized to do yoga

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.840
versus some sort of sham yoga,

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.240
then you would have the same
kind of expectation either way.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:34.320
But if you know going into it
you're going to get yoga now,

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:35.040
which may help,

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.600
or yoga in 6 months and you
end up in the waitlist control,

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.520
this may spuriously amplify
the difference in treatment effect

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:45.600
because people assigned
to the waitlist may expect

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.720
to not get better
without active treatment.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.680
For example, here's a study
for yoga on urinary incontinence.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:57.520
Women were randomized to 6 weeks
of yoga or a waitlist control group.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:00.280
After 6 weeks, the incontinence
frequency decreased

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.480
by 70% in the yoga therapy

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:05.800
versus only 13% fewer episodes
in the control group.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:08.400
But it's possible that the women
in the yoga group reported

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.720
some benefits due to what's
called an expectation bias

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:13.960
because they expected
yoga to be helpful.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.160
So based on the body
of evidence, one might say

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.280
there's insufficient good quality
evidence to judge

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:22.760
whether yoga is useful for
women with urinary incontinence.

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:26.080
But what we'd like to see
are studies comparing yoga

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:29.720
to time-and-attention
control interventions ---

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:31.160
some alternative to yoga

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:33.040
that entails the same amount
of time and attention,

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.680
so people don't even know
they're in a control group.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:37.840
Now the question arises:

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.680
who cares if the benefits
from a yoga intervention

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:43.200
are due to the placebo effect
or expectation bias?

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.080
A benefit is a benefit.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:47.920
As long as people are
getting better, who cares?

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:50.800
But it would be nice to know
if yoga really helps or not,

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:52.960
and here we go.

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:55.560
Women with urinary incontinence
were randomly assigned

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:59.480
to a yoga group or a rigorous
time-and-attention control group

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.120
involving nonspecific
muscle stretching

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:04.480
and strengthening exercises.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:07.560
There are two different types
of urinary incontinence:

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:11.640
urgency incontinence, defined
as an involuntary loss of urine

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.360
associated with a sudden
strong desire to urinate,

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:17.640
or stress incontinence,
where an activity

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:21.720
such as sneezing
triggers an involuntary leak.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:24.880
Three months of yoga didn't
seem to significantly help

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:26.720
overall incontinence,

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:28.880
but it did seem to help
with stress incontinence,

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:32.480
decreasing by an average
of 61% in the yoga group

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:35.040
versus only 35% in controls

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:39.320
with no significant changes
in urgency incontinence.

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:40.200
In our next study,

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:44.360
we look at how sham acupuncture
underscores the necessity

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:48.200
of controlling for
expectancy effects.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:52.361
Yoga as therapy:
When is it helpful?

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.960
The scientific literature on yoga
is limited in scope and quality.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:59.400
I've talked about the long list
of issues that plague

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:00.920
so many of the yoga trials,

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.360
but let's continue down
the list of conditions

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.600
to get a sense of the best
available balance of evidence.

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:10.880
For example, yoga as a therapy
for irritable bowel syndrome.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:14.600
Evidence from randomized controlled
trials found that yoga beat out drugs

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:17.800
and was equally effective as
moderate-intensity walking,

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:21.120
so whichever form
of exercise you prefer.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:24.600
What about yoga for
Inflammatory Bowel Disease?

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:28.240
Since stress can be a trigger,
might yoga help?

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:31.320
In a trial of yoga as an add-on
therapy for adolescents

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:34.960
with inflammatory bowel disease,
it didn't seem to help at all.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:38.240
But in a trial of yoga
vs written self-care advice

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:41.920
for adults with ulcerative colitis,
12 weeks of yoga

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:44.800
induced a stronger increase
in quality of life,

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:46.640
and also reduced disease activity

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:49.422
even three months
after the study ended.

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:52.800
Now, the so-called self-care
group was really just handed

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:54.840
a couple general self-care books,

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:59.360
so the two treatments, yoga vs
self-care, were not matched at all

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:04.360
for time intensity, feeling part
of a group or for therapist contact---

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:07.560
each of which are likely to have
the "yoga" arm more likely

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:10.800
to nonspecifically improve
their quality of life.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:13.400
So, yes, yoga might be effective
in improving quality

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:16.760
of life in ulcerative colitis,
however, the benefits may be

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:20.200
no different to other group
exercise, for example.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:23.320
Indeed, there have been 11
human studies on the influence

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:26.560
of moderate exercise programs
upon disease activity

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:28.520
in chronic inflammatory
bowel disease,

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:35.000
and all 11 report benefit in terms
of reduced disease activity.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:38.720
Using passive controls doesn't
account for expectancy effects,

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:42.120
attention given to the study
subjects, and the time spent,

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:44.800
the investment that
the participants put in.

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:46.840
Here's an excellent
example of why having

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:49.680
more active control groups
is so important.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.840
This details a series of studies
on nonpharmacological

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:55.600
interventions for
menopausal hot flashes.

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:58.240
For example, randomized
to 8 weeks of acupuncture,

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:02.640
8 weeks of sham acupuncture,
or nothing, just usual care.

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.840
Sham acupuncture is when
they still stick needles in you,

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:09.520
but shallowly and not in
traditional acupuncture points.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:12.440
There was also a study
of 10 weeks of yoga classes,

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:16.480
or the attention control (health
and wellness education classes)

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:18.240
which lasted just
as long as the yoga

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:21.040
and were done in the same kind
of group social setting.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:22.840
Here's what they found.

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:26.120
The hot flashes in the usual care
group, the passive control

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:28.880
where they didn't do anything
special stayed pretty stable.

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:32.520
But the real acupuncture
worked way better,

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:35.424
But so did the
sham acupuncture.

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:39.160
So yoga and acupuncture
both worked just as well,

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:42.680
but so did the non-yoga
and fake acupuncture.

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:45.440
There just appeared to be
a general placebo effect

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:47.840
such that women in
all five intervention groups

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:50.720
benefited simply
by being in a study.

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:52.880
That's why it's always better
to have the control groups

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:55.440
do something, so you can
have some confidence

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:59.320
at least some of the effect
of your intervention is real.

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:02.080
But hey, at least
they had control groups.

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:05.400
This study, "Twelve-Minute
Daily Yoga Regimen Reverses

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:09.520
Osteoporotic Bone Loss"
caused quite a stir.

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:11.200
We know that when
it comes to bone health,

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:16.880
it's use it or lose it: exercise
early and exercise often.

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:19.120
Physical activity is
a widely accessible, low cost,

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:22.520
and highly modifiable
contributor to bone health.

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:25.000
Exercise transmits forces
through the skeleton,

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:28.680
generating signals that are detected
by your bone building cells.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:31.120
This is why the National
Osteoporosis Foundation,

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:33.000
the International
Osteoporosis Foundation,

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.160
and other agencies recommend
weight-bearing exercises

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:39.080
for the prevention of osteoporosis.

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:42.160
These include high impact
exercises, such as jumping,

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:45.520
aerobics, and running,
as well as lower impact exercises,

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:47.280
such as walking and weight training,

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:51.280
to create these mechanical signals
that spark bone growth.

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:53.600
Lower impact activities
such as yoga

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:56.640
are generally not considered
bone-building.

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:00.200
That's why the results
of this study were so surprising.

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:02.640
It was a study of
internet-recruited volunteers

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:06.720
comparing bone mineral density
changes before and after yoga.

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:10.880
The researchers devised
a 12-minute DVD of 12 yoga poses

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:13.960
that they believed would stimulate
increased bone density

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:16.993
in the spine and both
parts of the hip.

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:21.560
But in the end, out of the 741
patients recruited at the beginning,

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:26.280
only a few dozen actually sent in
their bone scans as instructed.

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:28.600
So that's only like 5%,

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:32.720
so they lost 95% of the people
that originally started.

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:35.200
You can totally imagine how
those who got positive results

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:36.920
were more likely
to send them in,

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:40.040
and the others that failed
just kind of slunk away,

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:43.240
so I don't consider this
convincing evidence.

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:45.160
Now the authors
had the attitude of

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:47.280
"Hey, look, so what do
you got to lose?

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:51.400
The side effects of yoga are all
good, so why not give it a try?"

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:54.640
Well, what you have to lose
is the opportunity

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:58.560
to do higher impact exercise
that has more decisive evidence

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:00.520
of bone benefit.

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:04.680
And all the side effects
are not necessarily good.

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:07.160
There have been vertebral
compression fractures

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:08.720
associated with yoga.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:11.160
In this series of nine cases
from the Mayo Clinic

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:15.000
they described spinal compression
fractures occurring a month to years

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:19.760
after initiating yoga-associated
spinal flexion exercises.

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:22.920
Both scientific and media reports
continue to advertise yoga

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:27.160
as a bone protective activity,
but there's a need for selectivity

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:30.863
in yoga poses in populations
at increased fracture risk.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:33.160
And four of the nine patients
developed a fracture

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:36.320
in the setting of a normal or
near-normal bone mineral density.

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:40.080
So maybe everyone should
be careful with these.

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:42.880
Yoga-related injuries
are not uncommon,

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:46.040
resulting in thousands of
emergency room visits a year,

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:49.960
and approximately 5% of those
are coming in with fractures.

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:52.880
I'll try to put that
injury rate in context,

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:56.479
comparing yoga
to other physical activities next.

00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:59.360
Even though for certain
health conditions

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:01.080
we have not reached
a point where we can say

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:04.680
there's good scientific evidence
that yoga is beneficial,

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:07.440
many patients do seem to benefit.

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:09.480
And isn't that what matters?

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:11.280
I mean even in the studies
that show the benefit

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:15.232
is mainly a placebo effect,
does this really matter?

00:13:15.232 --> 00:13:17.160
I mean, after all,
this editorial asserted,

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:20.920
yoga therapy has never been
shown to have adverse effects.

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:24.040
But that's simply not true.

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:26.840
About 2,000 yoga-related
injuries have been seen

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:31.200
in hospital emergency rooms
every year from 2001 to 2014.

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:35.200
It looks like only one fatality
has ever been reported

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:37.760
in the medical literature,
but if you look at the case report,

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:40.840
a 16-year-old girl collapsing
with a ruptured lung

00:13:40.840 --> 00:13:44.480
following voluntary mouth-to-mouth
yoga breathing exercises

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:47.360
with a teen-age boy in the hall ---

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:51.920
that can hardly be characterized
as a typical yoga practice.

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:56.120
There are tragic rare cases
tied to actual yoga,

00:13:56.120 --> 00:14:01.400
like this quad rupture in a young
woman, or God, a femoral fracture.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:04.180
The femur is the bone in your thigh --

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:06.520
longest, strongest and
heaviest bone in the body.

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:08.400
Typically, you only see
femur fractures

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:11.080
with trauma such as car crashes,

00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:14.720
but here it was just a guy
practicing this yoga posture

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:18.600
when he heard a large cracking
sound and felt immense pain

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:20.040
because, yikes,
this is what happened.

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:23.520
Now two weeks earlier
he had felt pain in his thigh

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:25.800
that he just thought
was muscle strain,

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:29.040
but bone biopsies showed
evidence of previous microfractures.

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:31.560
So it sounds like he had
been overdoing it,

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:34.520
weakening it before
the final crack.

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:36.720
So I guess the bottom line
is that people should listen

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:40.440
to their bodies and not
push themselves too far.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:41.320
Now, in my last video
I talked about the risk

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:43.840
of spinal compression fractures,
particularly those

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:47.920
with weakened bones,
osteopenia or osteoporosis.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:51.600
Poses causing hyperflexion
and hyperextension of the spine

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:53.840
should probably be avoided.

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:56.840
These were the poses tied
to injury in a few dozen cases

00:14:56.840 --> 00:15:00.920
of musculoskeletal injuries
described at the Mayo Clinic.

00:15:00.920 --> 00:15:03.800
But case reports and
series of case reports

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:05.880
are really just kind of
glorified anecdotes.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:10.240
I mean, they don't give you an
overall sense of the safety of yoga.

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:12.520
If you look at randomized
controlled yoga trials,

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:15.600
there's actually no significant
difference in injury rates

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:17.880
between the yoga groups
and the control groups

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:21.360
that used some other sort
of exercise intervention.

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:23.720
Now, this may not
necessarily translate out

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:27.040
into real-world experience,
given the short duration

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:30.920
of yoga in these clinical trials --
as little as a single day --

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:33.840
and the fact that they may be
more closely supervised

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:36.160
with more highly trained teachers.

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:38.400
That's why large-scale surveys
like this can be helpful,

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:41.640
looking at thousands of people
taking yoga classes.

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:45.920
Looks like about 30% of yoga
class attendees had experienced

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:47.800
some type of adverse event,

00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:50.440
most commonly just something
mild like muscle aches,

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:53.200
but some individuals did
experience more severe events

00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:55.480
which caused them to stop going.

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:59.360
Many causes were associated
with overexertion and overdoing

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:02.760
and starting out in
poor physical condition.

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:05.840
This systematic review included
nine observational studies

00:16:05.840 --> 00:16:11.000
with 9000+ yoga practitioners
and 9000+ non-yoga practitioners

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:14.640
from the US, Europe,
Asia, and Australia.

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:18.320
A considerable proportion of yoga
practitioners experienced injuries

00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:19.960
or other adverse events;

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:21.720
however most were
mild and transient

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:26.160
and risks were comparable to
those of non-yoga practitioners.

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:29.680
The risk of yoga-associated
injuries was estimated

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:33.720
as 1.45 per thousand hours
of yoga practice,

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:37.560
much lower than in higher-impact
sports activities such as soccer.

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:39.600
Compared to 1.5 for yoga,

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:42.880
runners have like 2.5 injuries
per thousand hours,

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:45.500
soccer 3.7,
5 for tennis,

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:51.360
and an hour of yoga is more
than 5 times safer than skiing.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:53.640
Here's where yoga falls
on the spectrum.

00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:58.920
Yoga appears much safer,
for example, than aerobic funk.

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:01.000
On the other hand, yoga
practitioners may suffer

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:04.080
an increased risk
of meniscus injury,

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:07.560
the main stabilizing and
cushioning cartilage in the knee.

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:10.240
Yoga was found associated
with significantly higher risk

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:13.640
of meniscus injury compared with
activities such as badminton,

00:17:13.640 --> 00:17:16.240
jogging, and climbing hills.

00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:19.560
Some fundamental yoga postures,
like the lotus position,

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:21.720
can be hard on the knees.

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:26.560
Yes, yoga poses can undoubtedly
improve the flexibility of the knee,

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:31.160
but immoderate joint movement
can pose a serious threat.

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:34.520
The bottom line is like any other
type of physical activity:

00:17:34.520 --> 00:17:36.680
yoga carries a risk of injury.

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:38.680
Exercise is indeed medicine,

00:17:38.680 --> 00:17:42.280
but like any medicine, it must be
prescribed appropriately.

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:45.560
Many patients rely on their yoga
practice for stress reduction,

00:17:45.560 --> 00:17:47.160
so it's important to be mindful

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:50.240
that being injured
is the last thing you need.

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:53.280
The higher-risk yoga poses
appear to include

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:56.360
headstands, shoulder stands,
lotus and half lotus,

00:17:56.360 --> 00:17:59.040
forward bends, backward
bends, and handstands.

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:01.480
And so beginners
should beware,

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:03.520
with particular attention
paid to the spine

00:18:03.520 --> 00:18:07.600
as this is where the highest
numbers of injuries occur.

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:10.320
Hot yoga deserves
special mention.

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:13.360
The extreme heat and
intensity of Bikram yoga

00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:14.640
may make it inappropriate

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:17.800
for older adults and people
with medical conditions.

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:20.960
But there are case reports like
this one of sudden cardiac arrest

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:23.080
even in a healthy 35-year-old.

00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:27.080
Pregnancy is an especially
vulnerable time for heat exposure.

00:18:27.080 --> 00:18:29.080
With the increased risk
of spinal defects

00:18:29.080 --> 00:18:30.680
and possibly
other birth defects

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:33.480
among fetuses exposed
to excessive heat,

00:18:33.480 --> 00:18:37.560
pregnant women should avoid
practicing hot yoga during pregnancy.

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:42.120
Maternal hyperthermia, whether
from a sauna or electric blankets,

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:47.360
had nearly a 2-fold increased risk
of spinal and brain malformations.

00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:50.040
For example, pregnant women
who use hot tubs

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:51.720
increased the risk
of bearing babies

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:54.080
with their intestines
outside their bodies

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:57.800
or being born
without a brain.

00:18:57.800 --> 00:19:00.200
As any other physical
or mental practice,

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:02.040
yoga should be practiced carefully

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:04.680
under the guidance
of a qualified instructor.

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:06.800
Beginners should avoid
extreme practices

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:10.320
such as headstand, lotus
position, forceful breathing.

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:12.440
Individuals with
medical preconditions

00:19:12.440 --> 00:19:14.640
should work with their
physician and yoga teacher

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:17.200
to appropriately adapt postures.

00:19:17.200 --> 00:19:20.920
For example, patients with glaucoma
should avoid upside-down positions,

00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:25.360
and patients with compromised bones
should avoid forceful yoga practices,

00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:28.320
and practices like
voluntary vomiting

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:31.480
should perhaps be
avoided completely.

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:32.480
What?

00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:37.605
It's evidently a common
practice in traditional yoga.

